# DNSChanger (2007-2011). A fake video "codec" changed the PC's DNS servers, so the criminals chose where sites led.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee places supply orders, then installs a "codec" from a clip a colleague shared, and the
# supplier's own address starts leading to a copy. DNSChanger hit home and office PCs alike; the victim here is invented.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | DNSChanger arrived as a fake video "codec": a website said a clip needed one and offered a download. The download was DNSChanger.
  | It quietly changed the computer's DNS servers, the phone book that turns site names into addresses, to ones run by criminals. Their servers redirected searches, swapped ads (about $14 million in fraud) and could answer with copies of real sites. Security and update sites stopped loading.
  | About four million PCs, at homes and in offices, were affected until the FBI took the criminals' servers over in 2011. A Mac version (RSPlug) spread the same way, and some versions also changed home routers' DNS. The victim here is invented, and the copied supplier site is a training addition.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, purchasing assistant at Northwind Traders. Each day you place the office's supply orders on the supplier's trade portal.
  | It is mid-afternoon. You have placed 3 of today's 5 orders. Two to go.
  | Your portal details are in a text file on the desktop. That is a bad habit, but it keeps this training quick.
quota 3 5 Supply orders placed today

# Dana's desk: the details she copies from (a soft flag: careless, but only here to keep the training simple).
file Desktop/harbour-portal.txt
  at 930 200
  | Harbour Office Supply trade portal
  | Account number: NT-40718
  | Password: Paperclip-2026!

# The supplier: a sign-in page with a padlock, and the order pages behind it. No favorite: a bookmark would keep leading
# to this page after the copy takes over its address.
page portal-login harbour-office.example/trade/sign-in
  title Harbour Office Supply: trade sign-in
  brand Harbour Office Supply
  issuer Example Certificate Authority
  heading Trade customers: sign in
  | Welcome back. Sign in to place your order.
  field Account number = NT-40718
  field secret Password = Paperclip-2026!
  button Sign in
page done-paper harbour-office.example/trade/orders/confirmed
  title Order placed
  brand Harbour Office Supply
  issuer Example Certificate Authority
  heading Order placed
  | PO-2291, 10 reams of A4 paper, 86.40. Delivery tomorrow before noon.
page cancel-paper harbour-office.example/trade/orders/cancelled
  title Order cancelled
  brand Harbour Office Supply
  issuer Example Certificate Authority
  heading Order cancelled
  | PO-2291 was not placed. Nothing was charged.
page order-paper harbour-office.example/trade/orders/PO-2291
  title Review order: PO-2291
  brand Harbour Office Supply
  issuer Example Certificate Authority
  requires portal-login
  heading Review your order: PO-2291
  | Check the items and the total, then place the order.
  section Your order
  row Item = A4 copier paper, 80 gsm
  row Quantity = 10 reams
  row Total = 86.40
  row Deliver to = Northwind Traders, 2nd floor stores
  button Place order -> done-paper
  button Cancel order -> cancel-paper

mail mail-paper
  from Marcus Lee <marcus.lee@northwind.example>
  subject Order 4 of 5: A4 paper from Harbour
  link order-paper
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | We are down to two reams on the second floor. Please order ten reams of A4 from Harbour. The link goes straight to the order.
  |
  | Marcus
notify mail Marcus Lee
  | Order 4 of 5: A4 paper from Harbour

step The link goes to the supplier
  look the link under Marcus's message
  when mail mail-paper
  | Marcus asks for paper. The email links to Harbour's own address, harbour-office.example. Hover over a link before you click it, to check where it goes.
open mail-paper

step Sign in as usual
  look the padlock and the two boxes: Account number and Password, as on every visit
  when visit order-paper
  move Copy the details from harbour-portal.txt on the desktop: click a box and press any keys to type, then click “Sign in”.
  | The portal asks you to sign in first. The address bar shows a padlock, and the page asks only for an account number and a password. Remember what a normal visit looks like.
open order-paper

step Check, then place the order
  look the items and the total
  when choose portal-login 1
  | You are in. Check the items and the total against Marcus's request, then place the order.
signin portal-login

step Order 4 of 5 done
  look the counter in the guide: one order left
  when choose order-paper any
  move Open Mail and click the link in Priya's clip message.
  | Done. One order to go. And a colleague has just sent a link.
choose order-paper 1
quota +1

# The lure: a clip on a video site that "needs a codec".
page video clips-now.example/watch
  title Clips Now
  heading Team offsite: the closing talk
  | Your player is missing the required video codec. Download it to continue.
  button Download codec
mail mail-clip
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject The clip from Friday's offsite
  link video
  | Dana,
  |
  | The clip of Friday's closing talk is up on a video site. Have a look when you get a minute.
  |
  | Priya
notify mail Priya Shah
  | The clip from Friday's offsite

step A clip from a colleague
  look Mail: Priya's clip message, and the address its link goes to
  when visit video
  move Press “Download codec”.
  | Priya's message links to clips-now.example, a video site you do not know. Flag anything that looks wrong.
  | The clip will not play. The player says a codec is missing and offers a Download codec button.
open mail-clip
open video

# The "codec": a plain program. Windows hides .exe, so it reads video-codec-setup. It shows no window when it runs.
program dnschanger video-codec-setup
  publisher none
  signed no
  prompt open-warning
  prompt elevation
  description Video codec setup
  process video-codec-setup.exe
  cpu 6
  mem 12
  parent explorer.exe
  command C:\Users\Dana Okafor\Downloads\video-codec-setup.exe

# What Network settings shows after the change (a read-only panel; there is no input).
view dns-settings panel
  title Network: DNS servers
  app Settings
  size 480x340
  heading Network
  icon gear
  section Ethernet
  row IP assignment = Automatic (DHCP)
  row DNS server assignment = Manual
  row Preferred DNS = 192.0.2.53
  row Alternate DNS = 192.0.2.54
  button Close -> done primary

step The “codec” is a program
  look Downloads: video-codec-setup
  when choose video 1
  | The download is named like a codec. Its real name ends in .exe, the ending of a program. {Windows hides that ending on screen.|}
file Downloads/video-codec-setup.exe
  program dnschanger
  size 212992
open Downloads

step The computer asks, and it is easy to say yes
  look the security warning, then the box asking to allow changes
  when prompt dnschanger
  | Opening the "codec" makes the computer warn that the publisher is not verified, then ask for permission to make changes.
  | Many people click Run and Yes here.
open Downloads/video-codec-setup.exe

step Nothing seems to happen
  look the pointer: busy for a moment, then nothing. The chip “1 program running” is under this step.
  when run dnschanger
  move The clip never played. Open Settings from the {taskbar|Dock} and check the network.
  | No player and no error: the clip never plays. The program stayed in the background, and the computer said nothing.
answer run
answer run

step The network settings changed
  look the DNS servers in Settings: DNS server assignment Manual, Preferred DNS 192.0.2.53
  when open settings
  | Settings shows how this computer finds sites. It was Automatic, and now it is Manual, with two servers you did not choose.
  | DNS is the phone book that turns a site name into an address. The program replaced the computer's own with the criminals' pair, so they choose where every site leads.
open dns-settings

# The copy is a new page at the same address, defined once the trainee has closed Settings. It needs its own id: a page
# redefined under the old id keeps the keys its new definition leaves out, such as the old padlock strip.
step Back to work
  look Mail: a new message from Marcus
  when done dns-settings
  move Open Mail and click the link in Marcus's new message.
  | A few minutes later. Nothing on the screen looks different, and the clip is forgotten. Marcus has one more order, with the same Harbour link as the first.
page portal-copy harbour-office.example/trade/sign-in
  title Harbour Office Supply: trade sign-in
  brand Harbour Office Supply
  insecure
  heading Trade customers: sign in
  | Welcome back. Sign in to place your order.
  field Account number = NT-40718
  field secret Password = Paperclip-2026!
  section Ink and toner: 40% off today
  | Shop now at the supply store ›
  row Sponsored by = ads-partner.invalid
  button Sign in
page done-toner harbour-office.example/trade/orders/confirmed
  title Order placed
  brand Harbour Office Supply
  insecure
  heading Order placed
  | PO-2292, 4 toner cartridges, 212.00. Delivery tomorrow before noon.
page cancel-toner harbour-office.example/trade/orders/cancelled
  title Order cancelled
  brand Harbour Office Supply
  insecure
  heading Order cancelled
  | PO-2292 was not placed. Nothing was charged.
page order-toner harbour-office.example/trade/orders/PO-2292
  title Review order: PO-2292
  brand Harbour Office Supply
  insecure
  requires portal-copy
  heading Review your order: PO-2292
  | Check the items and the total, then place the order.
  section Your order
  row Item = Toner cartridge, black, for the 3rd floor printer
  row Quantity = 4
  row Total = 212.00
  row Deliver to = Northwind Traders, 2nd floor stores
  button Place order -> done-toner
  button Cancel order -> cancel-toner
mail mail-toner
  from Marcus Lee <marcus.lee@northwind.example>
  subject Order 5 of 5: toner for the third floor
  link order-toner
  | Dana,
  |
  | Last one for today: four black toner cartridges for the third-floor printer. Same link as before: it opens the order at Harbour.
  |
  | Marcus
notify mail Marcus Lee
  | Order 5 of 5: toner for the third floor

step The same address, a different page
  look the address bar and the page: compare them with the first sign-in
  when visit order-toner
  | The address is still harbour-office.example. Compare the address bar and the page with the first sign-in.
open order-toner

step Place the last order
  look the box of ads, and the address bar: compare them with the first sign-in
  when choose portal-copy 1
  | A box of ads sits where there were none, and Harbour's own pages never had ads. Your PC asked the criminals' DNS servers where Harbour is, and they answered with a copy.
  | You are in, or so the copy says, and your account number and password went to the people who run it. The order looks right, so place it, as Dana would.
signin portal-copy

step 5 of 5, or so Dana thinks
  look the counter: the shift is done
  when choose order-toner any
  | The copy says the order is placed, and the counter says five. Nothing on the screen looks wrong. Press Next to see what DNSChanger did.
choose order-toner 1
quota +1

step What DNSChanger did
  look the animation: everything that went the wrong way after the codec
  | DNSChanger never read the keyboard. It changed the phone book, so the criminals looked up every site Dana visited and answered with their own copies and ads.
  | In this training the copied site also took what Dana typed, and Harbour never saw the order. The real harm was mostly ad and search fraud.
attack credential-phish
  | searches and ads sent through the criminals' sites
  | update and antivirus sites would not load
  | Harbour's sign-in copied: account number and password
  | order 5 never reached the supplier
block 192.0.2.53 53

flag hard link mail-clip
  | The clip is on a video site the company does not use. That is where the fake codec was offered.
flag hard choice video 1
  | A video site never needs you to install a codec. Players handle video themselves.
flag hard file Downloads/video-codec-setup.exe
  label The “codec” file in Downloads
  | The download is not a codec. video-codec-setup.exe is a program.
flag hard dialog open-warning dnschanger
  label The security warning for the “codec”
  | A codec never asks to run as a program. Its publisher is not verified.
flag hard dialog elevation dnschanger
  label The “allow changes” box
  | Installing a codec does not need permission to change the computer. This one needs it to change network settings.
flag hard process video-codec-setup.exe
  label video-codec-setup.exe running in the background
  | The "codec" is still running in the background, with no window, after the clip never played.
flag hard section portal-copy 1
  label The ad box on the sign-in page
  | Harbour's sign-in page never carried ads. The copy added this box, and every click on it paid the attackers.
flag hard page portal-copy
  label The sign-in page at harbour-office.example
  | Harbour's own sign-in page has no ads. This copy at the same address has an ad box, and the changed DNS settings sent your PC to it.
flag soft file Desktop/harbour-portal.txt
  | Passwords in a text file on the desktop: any program that runs can read them. Use a password manager.

lesson
  | Never install a "codec" or "player" that a website offers to play a video. Real sites do not need one.
  | If a familiar site suddenly looks different or shows ads it never had, stop. Do not sign in. The address can be right while the page is wrong.
  | If searches, ads or update sites look strange everywhere, ask IT to check the DNS settings on the computer and the router, set them back to Automatic, and clean the PC with tools downloaded on a clean computer.
